Adaptive Mobile Agent Orchestration under Resource and Security Constraints: A Formal Framework for Falsifiable Evaluation

Ouadi Maakoul
SI TEAM

Working paper v0.4 — pre-registration draft

---

Status Declaration

This document is a theoretical framework and pre-registration protocol. No experiments have been conducted. No models have been trained. No physical device tests have been performed. No code has been executed. Every empirical claim is stated as a hypothesis to be tested under the protocol specified in Section 7. Every numerical threshold is stated as to be fixed by a future pilot study. Every reference to prior work is cited inline by author, year, and arXiv identifier where applicable.

---

Abstract

This paper formalizes a decision-theoretic framework for orchestrating AI agents on mobile devices. Rather than proposing an architecture and seeking to justify it, we formulate a constrained optimization problem whose candidate solution — an adaptive mode-selection policy termed AMR — is treated as a falsifiable hypothesis. The framework separates a mode-selection policy and an execution policy from an authorization monitor whose enforcement is independent of agent cooperation.

The v0.4 formulation incorporates validated corrections from a systematic review of recent work. First, the security-regret decomposition of Sun and Wang (arXiv:2608.06520) is cited as related work only, not as a formal grounding: their setting involves hidden Byzantine agents whose overwrites are unobservable to the learner, whereas AMR's monitor is deterministic and observable. Second, the pre-registration protocol adopts PRML v0.1 for single-metric threshold locking and pairs it with an OSF registration for the full statistical analysis plan, since PRML binds one metric to one threshold and cannot accommodate a mixed-effects plan with multiplicity control. Third, the contribution is positioned as a conditional extension of Bounded Agents (Muruaga, arXiv:2608.15888), which provides composition-sound authorization under serialized admission, and CAPMAS (Veski, Guerraoui, Froelicher, arXiv:2609.06500), which provides Macaroon-based monotone delegation under an honest-but-curious threat model. Fourth, the interface assumption between AMR's parallel execution policy and APC's serialized admission is stated explicitly, and the cost of serialization is declared as a measurable quantity. Fifth, four additional baselines are declared: HeRo (Li et al., arXiv:2603.01661), DAOEF, FAuNO, and AID-Guard (Tong, Dai, Guo, arXiv:2608.21159).

The contribution is stated as a research hypothesis, not a novelty claim. We identify the only cell of the novelty matrix that could remain open: the coupling of a mode-selection policy whose primary function is to decide when not to orchestrate, regret under compositional security constraints, and an authorization monitor within a single pre-registered protocol.

Keywords: mobile agents, adaptive orchestration, constrained optimization, regret analysis, compositional authorization, pre-registration, falsifiability.

