1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The deployment of AI agents on mobile devices is no longer primarily limited by computational power. Mobile NPUs, quantized models, and efficient inference runtimes are advancing steadily. What remains unsolved is coordination: deciding when orchestration is warranted, how to allocate resources across CPU, GPU, NPU, and cloud, how to compose authorizations among agents without enabling privilege escalation, and how to measure whether these decisions produce a real, reproducible, and bounded gain.

Three methodological temptations must be resisted at the outset.

Temptation 1 — Assuming orchestration is the bottleneck. On some devices, the dominant cost is inference; on others, memory bandwidth or radio. The first empirical task is to locate the bottleneck, not to assume it.

Temptation 2 — Assuming adaptation is beneficial. In a null regime (single task, fixed model, no contention), adaptation is pure overhead. A system that cannot decline to adapt has not demonstrated adaptivity; it has demonstrated a slogan.

Temptation 3 — Treating zero observed violations as security. Statistical bounds and formal verification answer different questions. Neither substitutes for the other, and neither is universal.

1.2 Relation to prior work

Authorization substrate. Bounded Agents (Muruaga, arXiv:2608.15888) introduces the Agentic Principal Chain (APC), which tracks delegated authority from one principal to the next, evaluates each request against accumulated session state using six authorization checks, carries forward and restricts delegated scope and budgets, and uses composition closure to prevent prohibited combinations. APC proves Blast Radius Monotonicity and Composition Soundness; Composition Soundness is limited to prohibited combinations under a complete restriction set and serialized admission. Its evaluation reports blocked exfiltration and destruction cases, with authorization latency of 0.24 ms at the 99th percentile on an idle host, and utility reductions of 8.6 and 13.9 percentage points across two AgentDojo settings. We treat APC as an inherited substrate, not a competitor.

Delegation layer. CAPMAS (Veski, Guerraoui, Froelicher, arXiv:2609.06500) combines contrastive learning-based semantic scoping with Macaroon-based tokens for offline, tamper-evident delegation with monotonic privilege reduction. It reports 30× faster delegation, 2× less latency, and up to 3× lower bandwidth than OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange; over 90% perfect privilege-bundle retrieval within 17 ms on enterprise schemas with over 3,100 endpoints; and 99.5% reduction in unnecessary privileges. Its threat model is honest-but-curious, targeting containment of non-deterministic LLM failures. Section 8 of that work explicitly discusses extension to malicious agents. We treat CAPMAS as an inherited delegation layer.

Attenuation formalism. Biscuit (via the AIP survey, arXiv:2603.24775) uses Datalog policies with Ed25519 signature chaining. The survey notes that Macaroon caveats are simple key-value predicates, insufficient for conditional policies involving temporal bounds, budget limits, and tool-parameter constraints, whereas Biscuit's Datalog attenuation blocks are strictly more expressive. We therefore treat Axiom D (monotone attenuation) as a constraint satisfied by an external token mechanism, not as a contribution of this paper.

Security regret. Sun and Wang (arXiv:2608.06520) prove that security regret decomposes into return regret and a cumulative response gap $D_K$, and that two indistinguishable horizon-one instances force $\Omega(K)$ expected security regret while return regret is zero. In their setting, hidden Byzantine agents overwrite planned actions after observing the plan; the learner observes planned actions and public rewards but neither the overwrite nor the executed joint action. We cite this work as related, not as formal grounding. AMR's monitor is deterministic and observable; $D_K$ is a different object from the price of security we define. The distinction is elaborated in Section 4.2.

Mobile orchestration. HeRo (Li et al., arXiv:2603.01661, DAC 2026) provides heterogeneity-aware RAG orchestration on mobile SoCs, with profiling-based performance models for latency, workload shape, and contention-induced slowdown, and an online scheduler combining shape-aware sub-stage partitioning, criticality-based accelerator mapping, and bandwidth-aware concurrency control. It reports up to 10.94× latency reduction on commercial mobile devices. Additional baselines are declared in Section 7.2.

Authorization overhead. AID-Guard (Tong, Dai, Guo, arXiv:2608.21159) reports a stateful authorization-to-effect closure protocol that blocked all submitted attacks under complete proposer compromise while admitting matched legitimate proposals, but with a substantial benign-utility reduction under a strict exact-manifest profile. This is direct precedent for treating authorization cost as a measurable quantity, not a negligible overhead.

Non-stationary online learning. Zhao, Wang, and Zhou (AISTATS 2022, PMLR 151:2101–2133) and its JMLR extension (Zhao, Yan, Wang, Zhou, JMLR 24(206):1–70, 2023) study tracking regret and adaptive regret under switching costs. The causal $K$-switch class defined in Section 4 is the natural domain of this literature. We adopt the switching-cost term $\lambda D \sum_t \|p_t - p_{t-1}\|_1$ as the abstract analogue of our per-switch cost $\kappa$, while noting that in AMR $\kappa$ carries physical units (joules, milliseconds), not abstract regret.

We state the candidate contribution narrowly: not "orchestrating mobile agents," but the coupling, within a single pre-registered protocol, of (a) a mode-selection policy whose primary purpose is to decide when not to orchestrate, (b) a regret analysis under compositional security constraints, and (c) an authorization monitor whose invariants are admissibility conditions. Whether this coupling is novel remains an empirical question.

1.3 Contributions of this chapter

1. A formal problem statement separating admissibility (security invariants) from optimization (performance under energy, latency, and quality constraints).
2. A two-policy plus monitor architecture, with the mode-selection policy distinguished from a policy-generating meta-policy.
3. A regret framework with reference classes defined by their information and constraints, over fixed experimental episodes, with clairvoyant values defined as expectations of maxima over realized episode paths, not maxima of expectations.
4. Correctly oriented definitions of price of security and price of causality on optimal values, with the D_K analogy treated as related work rather than formal grounding.
5. A complete mode-transition specification, including entry, exit, fallback, dwell, and hysteresis rules.
6. A compositional authorization predicate over the full monitor state, including P3c in the admissibility set.
7. Explicit forbidden-history automata for exfiltration and budget-fragmentation classes, with regular-closure justification.
8. Hypotheses H_abstain, H_cost, H_stability, H1, H2, H3 with pre-registered refutation criteria and a declared one-sided $\alpha$ for the non-inferiority test.
9. A pre-registrable protocol adopting PRML v0.1 for single-metric threshold locks and OSF registration for the full statistical analysis plan.
10. An explicit interface statement between AMR's parallel execution policy and APC's serialized admission, with serialization cost declared as a measurable quantity.

