4. Regret framework with declared information structure

4.1 Why regret over fixed episodes

The regret framework must specify, for each reference class, what the reference knows and how it is evaluated. Otherwise the comparison mixes distinct effects: informational limits, constraint costs, and switching costs. All references are evaluated on the same episode: same initial tasks, same budget, same time limit, same stopping rule, same transition model.

4.2 Relation to the security-regret literature

Sun and Wang (arXiv:2608.06520, preprint) prove that security regret decomposes exactly as

\operatorname{Reg}^{\mathrm{sec}}_{K} = \operatorname{Reg}_{K} + D_{K}, \qquad D_{K} \coloneqq \sum_{k=1}^{K}\{m_{k} - W(\pi_{k})\},

with $D_K \geq 0$. Their setting involves hidden Byzantine agents that overwrite planned actions after observing the plan; the learner observes planned actions and public rewards but neither the overwrite nor the executed joint action. The response gap $D_K$ is unobservable to the learner.

We cite this as related work only. The price of security defined below is a constraint-relaxation gap: the difference between the optimal value achievable under security constraints and the optimal value achievable in a sandboxed simulator where those constraints are relaxed. It is a shadow price, not an unobserved adversary gap. The symbolic overlap with $D_K$ is structural; the objects are distinct. In particular, the switch count $K$ in this paper counts mode transitions, whereas in Sun and Wang it counts episodes. Conflating the two would misstate the analogy.

4.3 Reference classes

Definition 4.1 (Reference class). A reference class $\mathcal{P}$ is a set of policies with a declared information set $\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{P})$ and a declared constraint set $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P})$. Each reference policy is evaluated by replaying it in a calibrated simulator on episode $e$ — never by attributing to it the rewards observed under another policy.

Four reference classes are used:

Reference Authorized information Constraints Role
Fixed policy $\mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}}$ Observed state; constant strategy Full security $\mathcal{C}_{\text{sec}}$, full budget $B$ Measure the benefit of adaptation
Causal $K$-switch $\mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}$ Available history; at most $K$ switches; per-switch cost $\kappa$ Full security Realistic adaptive comparator
Clairvoyant secured oracle $\mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}}$ Known future trajectory; at most $K$ switches Full security Diagnostic upper bound under constraints
Clairvoyant free oracle $\mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}$ Known future trajectory Relaxed security Measure the theoretical cost of constraints

Definition 4.2 (Switch counts and costs). Two distinct switch counters are declared:

· $K_{\text{meta}}$ counts mode transitions of $\mu$, with cost $\kappa_{\text{meta}}$ measured as the controller's energy and latency during transition.
· $K_{\text{exec}}$ counts fine-grained execution changes (placement changes, preemptions, parallelism changes), with cost $\kappa_{\text{exec}}$ measured as migration energy and re-scheduling latency.

The total switching cost is $\kappa_{\text{meta}} K_{\text{meta}} + \kappa_{\text{exec}} K_{\text{exec}}$. Regret is reported separately for both definitions of $K$ as a sensitivity analysis. Reporting only $K_{\text{meta}}$ would hide the cost of continuous execution churn during stable meta-modes, as occurs in mixing-cost regimes of fixed-share algorithms.

Assumption 4.1 (nesting). The reference classes are nested:

\mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}.

Nesting is required for the price-of-security and price-of-causality interpretations to hold.

4.4 Regret definitions

Static regret (against best fixed policy):

R_{\text{static}} \;=\; \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}}} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_p(B) \,\right] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_\mu(B) \,\right].

This is the minimum bar AMR must clear to justify its existence.

Causal $K$-switch regret:

R_K^{\text{causal}} \;=\; \max_{p_{1:T} \in \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_{p_{1:T}}(B) \,\right] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_\mu(B) \,\right].

This is the realistic adaptive competitor. Causal references do not see the future.

Clairvoyant secured regret:

R_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \;=\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \max_{p_{1:T} \in \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}}} C_{p_{1:T}}(e) \right] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_\mu(B) \,\right].

The clairvoyant value is an expectation of a maximum over realized episode paths, not a maximum of expectations. These differ in general. The maximum-of-expectations form would correspond to a policy that selects the best-in-expectation reference and then plays it; the expectation-of-maximum form corresponds to a reference that adapts to each realized episode with full knowledge of that episode's trajectory. The latter is the correct diagnostic bound.

Clairvoyant free regret:

R^{\text{clair,free}} \;=\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}} C_p(e) \right] \;-\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_\mu(B) \,\right].

4.5 Price of security and price of causality

The v0.1 formulation compared regret differences, which is not, in general, the price of security. We compare optimal values instead. Define:

V_{\text{fix}}^\star \;=\; \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}}} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_p(B) \,\right],

V_K^{\text{causal}} \;=\; \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\, C_p(B) \,\right],

V_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \;=\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \max_{p_{1:T} \in \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}}} C_{p_{1:T}}(e) \right],

V^{\text{clair,free}} \;=\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}} C_p(e) \right].

Then:

\operatorname{PoS} \;=\; V^{\text{clair,free}} \;-\; V_K^{\text{clair,sec}},

\operatorname{PoC} \;=\; V_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \;-\; V_K^{\text{causal}},

\operatorname{VoA} \;=\; V_K^{\text{causal}} \;-\; V_{\text{fix}}^\star.

The price of security is the loss attributable specifically to the security constraints, holding information and switching budget fixed. The price of causality is the loss attributable to the informational constraint. The value of adaptation is the maximum attainable improvement from any causal adaptive strategy over the best fixed policy.

Assumption 4.2 (interpretability). These three quantities are interpretable as the stated effects only if:

1. the reference classes are nested (Assumption 4.1);
2. the objective is identical across classes;
3. budgets and resource constraints are identical;
4. the oracles are defined over the same transition model;
5. all references are evaluated in the same calibrated simulator on the same episodes.

Without these conditions, the differences are arithmetic artefacts.

4.6 Oracle evaluation in a calibrated simulator

Physical replay of counterfactual policies on the same mobile device is impossible: energy consumed early modifies thermal state, memory pressure, and battery trajectory, invalidating the second replay. Consequently:

Assumption 4.3 (calibrated simulator). All oracle reference values are computed in a simulator calibrated against pilot measurements of the target device. Calibration covers at minimum: inference energy per model per placement, memory-bandwidth energy, radio energy per byte and per idle second, thermal throttling onset, and battery discharge curve. Calibration residuals are reported. Regret values computed against an uncalibrated simulator are not admissible as evidence for H1 or H2.

Remark 4.1 (how the non-secure oracle is evaluated). The clairvoyant free oracle is not simulated as a policy that performs forbidden actions in a real system. It is evaluated in the same calibrated simulator with the security constraints relaxed. Its value is a mathematical bound, not a claim about achievable performance.

