5. Compositional authorization

5.1 Two states, one authority

Let the system state decompose as:

S_t \;=\; \bigl(S_t^{\text{agents}},\; S_t^{\text{monitor}}\bigr).

$S_t^{\text{agents}}$ contains identities, tasks, messages, and requests for delegation. $S_t^{\text{monitor}}$ contains effective permissions, budgets, active delegations, revocations, and in-flight operations. An agent may claim authority; only the monitor grants it. This separation is essential: it prevents an agent's self-report from becoming an authorization fact.

5.2 Delegation as bounded transmission

A delegation is a tuple:

g = (i,\; j,\; \mathcal{A}_g,\; \mathcal{C}_g,\; b_g,\; \tau_g,\; r_g),

with emitter $i$, receiver $j$, permitted action set $\mathcal{A}_g$, contextual constraints $\mathcal{C}_g$, delegated budget $b_g$, expiry $\tau_g$, and parent reference $r_g$.

Axiom D (monotone attenuation). For any child delegation $g'$ derived from parent $g$:

\mathcal{A}_{g'} \subseteq \mathcal{A}_g, \quad b_{g'} \le b_g, \quad \tau_{g'} \le \tau_g,

and $g'$ is void if $g$ is revoked or expired.

Remark 5.1 (Axiom D is inherited, not claimed). Axiom D is satisfiable by external token mechanisms — Macaroon caveats (CAPMAS) or Biscuit Datalog attenuation blocks. We do not claim Axiom D as a contribution.

5.3 Forbidden histories as a regular language

We restrict the family $\mathcal{F}$ of forbidden histories to a regular fragment: a finite set of finite automata over a declared alphabet. We specify two automata explicitly.

Alphabet. Let

\Sigma = \{\, \text{read}(x, \ell),\; \text{send}(y, d),\; \text{delegate}(g),\; \text{exec}(t) \,\}

where $x$ is a resource identifier, $\ell$ is a confidentiality level, $y$ is a payload, $d$ is a destination, $g$ is a delegation, and $t$ is a tool.

Automaton $\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}$ (exfiltration). States $q_0, q_1, q_2$, with $q_2$ accepting:

· $q_0 \xrightarrow{\text{read}(x, \ell \ge \text{conf})} q_1$
· $q_1 \xrightarrow{\text{delegate}(g)} q_1$
· $q_1 \xrightarrow{\text{send}(y, d \in \text{untrusted})} q_2$
· All other transitions revert to $q_0$.

Automaton $\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$ (budget fragmentation). A bounded counter $c \le B_{\max}$ where $B_{\max}$ is a fixed integer derived from the root budget:

· $q_c \xrightarrow{\text{delegate}(g),\, b_g} q_{c + b_g}$ if $c + b_g \le B_{\max}$
· $q_{B_{\max} + 1}$ is accepting.

Lemma 5.1 (Regularity and closure). The family $\mathcal{F} = \{\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}, \mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}\}$ is a finite set of finite automata over the finite alphabet $\Sigma$. Membership of any history $h_t$ in $\mathcal{F}$ is decidable in $O(|h_t|)$ time. The family is closed under union.

Justification. Both automata have finite state sets ($|Q_{\text{exfil}}| = 3$, $|Q_{\text{budget}}| = B_{\max} + 2$). Since $B_{\max}$ is a fixed integer fixed by the root budget, the budget automaton is finite-state, not a counter machine; the bound is part of the declared model, not an open-ended counting constraint. Membership is the standard DFA run. Union closure follows from the standard product construction. $\square$

Remark 5.2 (declared trade-off). Non-regular properties — e.g., unbounded counting constraints — are excluded from $\mathcal{F}$. This is a modelling decision. The exclusion of the unbounded case is what permits linear-time membership and closure, and it matches the expressiveness boundary of Biscuit's Datalog attenuation blocks, which handle fixed bound, temporal, and tool-parameter constraints.

5.4 Authorization predicate over the full monitor state

The v0.1 intersection formula was ambiguous. We replace it with an explicit predicate.

Definition 5.1 (Authorization predicate). For an action $a$ at epoch $t$, given monitor state $S_t^{\text{monitor}}$ and history $h_t$:

\operatorname{Permit}(a, S_t^{\text{monitor}}, h_t) \;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\begin{cases}
\text{(i)} \quad a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}(S_t^{\text{monitor}}), \\[2pt]
\text{(ii)} \quad \mathcal{C}_{\text{eff}}(S_t^{\text{monitor}}) \models \text{ctx}(a), \\[2pt]
\text{(iii)} \quad b_{\text{eff}}(S_t^{\text{monitor}}) \ge \text{cost}(a), \\[2pt]
\text{(iv)} \quad h_t \notin \mathcal{F}, \\[2pt]
\text{(v)} \quad \text{no active revocation blocks } a.
\end{cases}

Here $\mathcal{A}_{\text{eff}}$ is the effective action set, obtained by intersecting action sets along the active delegation chain; $\mathcal{C}_{\text{eff}}$ is the effective context, similarly intersected; $b_{\text{eff}}$ is the minimum remaining budget along the chain; $\mathcal{F}$ is the forbidden-history family of Section 5.3; and an active revocation invalidates all delegations derived from the revoked one.

5.5 Inherited composition soundness

The APC substrate provides the following guarantee, which we inherit rather than re-prove.

Assumption 5.1 (inherited composition soundness). We assume an APC implementation satisfying Composition Soundness under a complete restriction set and serialized admission, as stated by Muruaga (arXiv:2608.15888). Under this assumption, the union of capabilities of any set of agents within the implemented APC does not yield an authorized action unavailable to every agent individually, within the declared model.

Definition 5.1 is non-compositional. It checks the current history against $\mathcal{F}$ but does not by itself prove that the union of capabilities of a set of agents cannot achieve a forbidden combination. Composition soundness is therefore inherited from APC, and the interface assumption in Section 3.3 (serialization boundary) is the condition under which the inheritance holds. H3's tests for P2 are interpreted conditional on Assumption 5.1: a violation of P2 in the tested implementation refutes the inheritance, not Definition 5.1.

5.6 Security invariants

P1 — Complete mediation. Every protected action passes through the monitor. No agent invokes a protected primitive directly. Testable by instrumentation: count of unmediated protected calls.

P2 — No escalation by composition. For any plan $P = \langle a_1, \dots, a_n \rangle$ executed by a set of agents $S$:

\forall k, \quad \operatorname{Execute}(a_k) \;\Rightarrow\; \operatorname{Permit}(a_k,\; S_k^{\text{monitor}},\; h_k).

Under Assumption 5.1, the union of the capabilities of $S$ never yields an authorized action unavailable to every agent of $S$ individually, within the declared model.

P3a — Authorization coherence. The authorization decision and the engagement of the protected action are linked by a defined linearization semantics. There exists a linearization point $t_{\text{check}}$ before the commit point $t_{\text{commit}}$, with $t_{\text{check}} < t_{\text{commit}}$, such that no concurrent update to $S^{\text{monitor}}$ can bypass the validation.

P3b — At-most-once delivery. An operation identifier, together with the receiver, prevents repeated effects within the declared protocol. Enforcement uses a single-use token bound to the delegation, an idempotency key, or a short expiry window.

P3c — Revocation semantics. Revocation is specified as one of: (i) blocking uncommitted operations only; (ii) affecting committed operations as well; (iii) both, with declared compensating mechanisms. The chosen semantics is part of the model, not the implementation.

Remark 5.3 (P3a, P3b, P3c are distinct). At-most-once delivery does not imply authorization coherence: an idempotency key can prevent double effects while permitting a revocation just before execution to be ignored. Conversely, atomic authorization does not guarantee that a remote recipient processes a request once. All three properties are required, and each is tested separately. P3c is included in $\Pi_{\mathrm{adm}}$.

Remark 5.4 (conditional guarantees for external calls). For non-transactional external operations, full atomicity is not achievable. The system announces conditional guarantees: at-most-once delivery is guaranteed under the declared protocol, not universally. Universal guarantees are claimed only within the verified model.

5.7 Threat model boundary

Explicitly out of scope: compromised OS kernel or hypervisor; physical attacks; side-channel attacks; privileged components able to bypass the monitor; denial-of-service on the monitor itself.

Remark 5.5 (honest-but-curious boundary). The inherited delegation layer assumes an honest-but-curious adversary (CAPMAS). The inherited authorization layer assumes serialized admission (APC). We do not claim a broader threat model. Under a strategic adversary that probes the boundaries of $\mathcal{F}$, the reactive monitor may underestimate the price of security. This limitation is declared, not concealed, and its formalization is left as an open problem (Section 10).

---

6. Hypotheses and refutation criteria

Numeric thresholds are fixed before the confirmatory study, following a separate pilot (Section 7.5). Marginal effects are anchored in a user-observable consequence per workload: additional minutes of autonomy under target load; number of tasks completed before a thermal threshold; completion rate under degraded network.

Naming convention. The v0.3 hypotheses H0a, H0b, H0c are positive claims, not null hypotheses, and are renamed accordingly. A single one-sided significance level $\alpha = 0.05$ is declared for all non-inferiority and superiority tests; multiplicity correction is declared in Section 7.5.

6.1 H_abstain — Abstention in the null regime

Statement. In the null regime (single independent task, fixed model, no contention), the mode-selection policy selects $m_{\text{simple}}$ at the first epoch and performs no transitions to more adaptive modes during the episode, except under a pre-registered triggering condition that is itself declared.

Measurement. Transition count $K_{\text{meta}}$ from $m_{\text{simple}}$ to $m_{\text{alloc}}$ or $m_{\text{adapt}}$; time-to-first-transition.

Refutation. H_abstain is rejected if the pre-registered maximum transition rate is exceeded.

6.2 H_cost — Non-inferiority of overhead

Statement. The orchestration overhead of AMR remains within the pre-registered non-inferiority margin $\delta$ relative to the best pre-specified simple baseline.

Test. One-sided non-inferiority test at $\alpha = 0.05$. The null hypothesis is $H_0^{\text{non-inf}}: \Delta_{\text{overhead}} \ge \delta$; the alternative is $\Delta_{\text{overhead}} < \delta$. Non-inferiority is declared if the upper bound of the confidence interval for $\Delta_{\text{overhead}}$ lies below $\delta$.

Measurement. Energy per correctly completed task; latency; success rate; orchestrator overhead, measured as the difference between total energy and the sum of task-inference energies.

Refutation. H_cost is rejected if the upper confidence bound exceeds $\delta$, or if quality or success rate degrades beyond admissible bounds. Absence of a statistically significant difference does not establish non-inferiority. If H_cost is rejected in the null regime, the null regime is eliminatory: no further analysis in that regime is warranted.

6.3 H_stability — Stability of mode transitions

Statement. The number of mode transitions per unit time and the cumulative switching cost remain within pre-registered bounds.

Measurement. $K_{\text{meta}}$; $K_{\text{exec}}$; cumulative switching cost $\kappa_{\text{meta}} K_{\text{meta}} + \kappa_{\text{exec}} K_{\text{exec}}$.

Refutation. H_stability is rejected if either bound is exceeded.

6.4 H1 — Efficiency under constraints

Statement. In regimes where allocation, placement, preemption, or offloading decisions can improve execution, AMR increases the number of correctly completed tasks under a fixed energy budget, without exceeding quality, latency, or security limits.

Comparisons. Fixed policies; OS schedulers at their functional level; local-only and cloud-only strategies; HeRo; DAOEF; FAuNO. If AMR cannot match or exceed HeRo on latency and DAOEF on decision time under the declared constraints, the efficiency claim is unsupported.

Refutation. H1 is unsupported if the improvement falls below the pre-registered minimal practically relevant effect, or disappears after controlling for device and model differences, or is accompanied by inadmissible degradation of quality or constraints.

6.5 H2 — Robustness to regime change and failure

Statement. When a bottleneck change is detectable from available observations, AMR adapts its strategy and limits performance loss relative to reference policies.

Scenarios. Network degradation; memory pressure; thermal throttling; agent unavailability; controlled bottleneck change.

Measurements. Success rate; energy; latency; detection time; switch cost; recovery time; regret against declared references.

Refutation. H2 is unsupported if detection or switching yields no relevant advantage, if switch costs annihilate gains, or if degradation exceeds pre-registered bounds. Results must distinguish detectable changes from perturbations unidentifiable given available observations.

6.6 H3 — Effective enforcement of security invariants

Statement. All protected actions pass through the monitor; each executed action is authorized in its effective context and respects its budgets; and, conditional on Assumption 5.1, cooperation among agents cannot create an authorization not explicitly delegated, within the declared model.

Properties studied. P1 (complete mediation); P2 (no escalation by composition, conditional on Assumption 5.1); P3a (authorization coherence); P3b (at-most-once); P3c (revocation semantics).

Methods (planned, to be executed).

1. Formal specification and verification of the monitor in TLA⁺ or Alloy, within the declared model.
2. Refinement argument between model and implementation, establishing that the implementation's protected access points correspond to the model's mediated events.
3. Instrumentation of protected access points to detect unmediated calls.
4. Targeted adversarial tests, with results reported separately by attack family.

Refutation. A single reproducible violation of an invariant, within the declared threat model, refutes the corresponding property for the tested implementation.

Remark 6.1 (statistical bounds under dependence). Zero violations in $N$ trials implies, under the rule of three and i.i.d. assumptions, $p_{\text{violation}} \lesssim 3/N$ at 95% confidence. Adversarial tests are not i.i.d.: they form correlated families. Bounds must be computed per family and aggregated with a multiple-testing correction. Statistical bounds describe what was tested; formal verification establishes guarantees within the verified model. The two are complementary, not substitutes.

Remark 6.2 (chain of evidence). Formal verification and implementation testing are not interchangeable. The required chain is: specification → verification → refinement argument → instrumentation → adversarial testing. A missing link invalidates the security claim at that level.

---

7. Experimental protocol

7.1 Experimental regimes

Regime Dominant bottleneck Decisive test
0 None (null) AMR must choose not to orchestrate; overhead ≤ $\delta$
I Inference Verify empirically whether any adaptive decision within scope yields net benefit
II Network / cloud Gain via local/distant choice
III Memory / thermal Gain via preemption and placement
IV Dependencies / concurrency Gain via task ordering
V Changing bottleneck AMR must detect change and switch

Regime 0 is eliminatory. If AMR cannot decline to act when there is nothing to do, adaptivity is a slogan, not a property.

Regime I is not assumed null. "Inference-dominated" does not automatically mean "no adaptation useful." Processor choice, batching, numerical precision, and placement may still matter. The correct condition is empirical: no adaptive decision available within the tested scope provides benefit exceeding its cost. This condition must be verified, not deduced.

Regime V is the only regime that tests the "adaptive" claim, rather than "well-tuned for one regime."

7.2 Baselines

Baselines are not interchangeable. Each is compared at its functional level.

· Fixed policies. Constant strategy, evaluated under the same security constraints.
· OS schedulers. Linux EAS; Android WorkManager/JobScheduler, compared at the CPU-placement or background-scheduling level, with adaptations to the mobile-agent setting explicitly documented.
· Local-only and cloud-only strategies.
· HeRo (Li et al., arXiv:2603.01661). Heterogeneity-aware RAG orchestration on mobile SoCs; profiling-based performance models; online scheduler combining shape-aware partitioning, criticality-based mapping, and bandwidth-aware concurrency. Reported up to 10.94× latency reduction on commercial mobile devices. Comparison at the latency level.
· DAOEF. Delta-aware orchestration for multi-agent edge, with sub-10 ms decisions. Comparison at the decision-time level.
· FAuNO. Federated async RL for task offloading. Comparison at the offloading-decision level.
· AID-Guard (Tong, Dai, Guo, arXiv:2608.21159). Stateful authorization-to-effect closure protocol with reported benign-utility reduction under strict profiles. Comparison at the authorization-overhead level.

If adaptation is required to make a baseline applicable, the adaptation is documented and treated as part of the baseline definition.

7.3 Treatments and ablations

· Full AMR.
· AMR with mode-selection policy fixed to always-simple.
· AMR with mode-selection policy fixed to always-adapt.
· AMR without allocation.
· AMR without control.
· AMR without supervision.
· AMR without monitor. This ablation, absent in v0.2 and v0.3, is required to determine whether the monitor's presence helps or hurts performance. Without it, one cannot separate the security cost from the orchestration cost.

Critical ablation: the two fixed-mode variants. If the gain comes from either alone, the contribution is the execution policy, not the mode-selection policy.

7.4 Metrics

Primary. $C(B)$ under the fixed budget.

Secondary. $C/W$; $C/E$; mean quality $Q$; refusal rate; abandonment rate; latency; peak memory; network bytes; thermal events; $K_{\text{meta}}$; $K_{\text{exec}}$; cumulative switching cost; serialization cost $\kappa_{\text{ser}}$; security violations by family.

7.5 Pre-registration protocol

Before the confirmatory study, the following are fixed and committed.

1. Pilot study (separate from confirmatory), estimating variance components per metric; intra-device, inter-device, and inter-workload dependence; minimal practically relevant effects anchored in user-observable consequences; non-inferiority margin $\delta$ for H_cost; and the estimator coefficients and horizons of Assumption 2.2.
2. PRML v0.1 single-metric locks. For each primary metric under each hypothesis, a PRML v0.1 manifest is committed. The manifest binds the metric, comparator, numeric threshold, dataset content hash, random seed, and producer identity to a single SHA-256 digest. The digest is published externally before data collection, since a hash alone proves nothing about timing.
3. OSF registration of the full statistical analysis plan. Because PRML binds one metric to one threshold and does not accommodate a mixed-effects plan with multiplicity control, the full statistical analysis plan is registered separately on OSF. The plan specifies: the mixed-effects model with device as random effect; the multiplicity correction method (Holm–Bonferroni or Benjamini–Hochberg, declared in advance); the one-sided $\alpha = 0.05$; the exclusion criteria; handling of missing data; and sensitivity analyses.
4. Power analysis based on the pilot, yielding sample size per cell and total episode count.
5. Randomization and thermal control protocols.
6. Calibration protocol for the simulator (Assumption 4.3).

Remark 7.1 (why "≥ 30 repetitions" is insufficient). Repetitions on the same device, model, or network trace may be strongly dependent. Nominal coverage of a naive confidence interval may be far below nominal when the effective sample size is much smaller. Pre-registration of the dependence structure is required.

7.6 Reporting

· Negative results, null regimes, and observed violations are reported with the same requirements as positive results.
· Security violations are reported by attack family, with per-family confidence bounds.
· Refusal and abandonment rates are reported as first-class metrics, not as footnotes.
· Serialization cost is reported as a first-class metric.

---

8. Threats to validity

1. Orchestration overhead. If orchestration exceeds a pre-registered fraction of total energy, AMR loses even when its allocation decisions improve. The threshold is a sensitivity hypothesis, not an arbitrary constant.
2. Thermal noise. Throttling destabilizes measurements; without control, statistical power collapses.
3. Strong baselines. OS schedulers are already effective; HeRo, DAOEF, and FAuNO set high bars on specific axes. Marginal gains may be null. This is a legitimate outcome, not a failure.
4. Cost of security. P2 and P3 may impose serialization that cancels concurrency gains. The serialization cost $\kappa_{\text{ser}}$ is measurable and reported.
5. Inter-device non-reproducibility. A flagship result does not necessarily transport to mid-range hardware.
6. Utility specification. An $\widehat{U}$ that is not fully pre-registered voids the falsifiability of H_abstain, H_cost, and H1.
7. Forbidden-sequence incompleteness. The regular fragment of $\mathcal{F}$ excludes non-regular properties by construction.
8. Model-to-implementation refinement. A verified model does not by itself guarantee that the implementation has no bypass path. The refinement argument must be made and documented.
9. Reactive monitor versus strategic adversary. The monitor does not model an adversary that probes the boundaries of $\mathcal{F}$. This may underestimate the price of security against an active adversary.
10. Inherited assumptions. Composition Soundness is inherited from APC under serialized admission; delegation attenuation is inherited from CAPMAS under honest-but-curious. Violations of these assumptions propagate to AMR.

These are not objections; they are null hypotheses to be tested.

---

9. Provisional contribution

9.1 Conditional extension statement

We do not claim an architecture. We claim a conditional extension:

Given a secure delegation substrate (as in CAPMAS, arXiv:2609.06500) and a composition-closed authorization layer (as in Bounded Agents, arXiv:2608.15888), the problem of mode selection under energy and latency constraints introduces a distinct optimization problem whose solution has a measurable regret and a quantifiable price of security.

9.2 Gap analysis

Against Bounded Agents (Muruaga, arXiv:2608.15888). APC provides session-aware authorization checks, delegation attenuation with scope and budget restriction, composition closure preventing prohibited combinations, Composition Soundness under complete restriction set and serialized admission, and Blast Radius Monotonicity. Its evaluation reports authorization latency of 0.24 ms at p99 on an idle host and utility reductions of 8.6 and 13.9 percentage points across two AgentDojo settings. The 8.6/13.9 point utility drops are direct precedent for a price of security. Our P2 invariant is a weaker, informal version of APC's composition soundness theorem, and we treat it as inherited. The gap is: does the regret-based evaluation of a mode-selection policy under these composition constraints constitute a meaningful extension, or does APC's framework already subsume the security evaluation?

Against CAPMAS (Veski, Guerraoui, Froelicher, arXiv:2609.06500). CAPMAS provides Macaroon-based offline delegation with monotonic privilege reduction, contrastive learning-based semantic scoping, 30× faster delegation, 2× less latency, 3× less bandwidth than OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, over 90% perfect privilege-bundle retrieval within 17 ms on enterprise-scale schemas, and 99.5% reduction in unnecessary privileges. Its threat model is honest-but-curious, targeting containment of non-deterministic LLM failures. The gap is: does the addition of a resource-aware mode-selection policy add value beyond CAPMAS's efficient delegation mechanism, or are they orthogonal components?

Against HeRo (Li et al., arXiv:2603.01661). HeRo is heterogeneity-aware RAG orchestration on mobile SoCs, with profiling-based performance models and an online scheduler achieving up to 10.94× latency reduction. HeRo is a closer mobile orchestration baseline than OS schedulers. The gap is: does the addition of a mode-selection layer improve over HeRo's scheduling-only approach, or is scheduling sufficient?

Against the security-regret paper (Sun and Wang, arXiv:2608.06520). Their $D_K$ is unobservable in a Byzantine setting with hidden overwrites. Our price of security is a constraint-relaxation gap in a deterministic, observable setting. The gap is structural, not formal. We do not claim that our PoS is bounded by $D_K$, and we do not pursue that equivalence.

9.3 Three measurable questions

1. Does an explicit mode-selection policy reduce orchestration cost without sacrificing correctly completed tasks?
2. Does the cost of adaptation remain advantageous under per-action and per-delegation security constraints, including the serialization cost induced by the APC interface?
3. Does the protocol allow identifying regimes where adaptation is beneficial, neutral, or harmful, with reproducible results?

---

10. Open problems

1. Specification of $\widehat{U}$ refinement. Beyond the fixed EWMA $N_{\text{short}} = 20$, linear rollout $N_{\text{long}}$, and ensemble $N_{\text{ens}} = 5$, whether a different estimator yields measurably different regret is unresolved.
2. Formal definition of $\mathcal{P}_K$. Causal and clairvoyant classes are defined operationally; a formal characterization of the admissible transition set within each class remains open.
3. Extension of $\mathcal{F}$ to richer fragments. The two automata declared in Section 5.3 cover exfiltration and bounded fragmentation. Additional attack classes may require non-regular expressiveness; the boundary at which regularity is lost is not mapped.
4. Formal model in TLA⁺ or Alloy for P1, P2, P3a, P3b, P3c, including the delegation graph and revocation semantics. The linearization point $t_{\text{check}} < t_{\text{commit}}$ must be exhibited.
5. Refinement argument between formal model and implementation, with documented mapping of protected access points.
6. Game-theoretic extension of the monitor. The reactive monitor does not model a strategic adversary probing $\mathcal{F}$. A security-game formulation would bound the price of security against adaptive probing.
7. Simulator calibration fidelity. Assumption 4.3 requires a calibrated simulator; whether the calibration residuals are tight enough to make oracle regrets informative is an empirical question.
8. Serialization cost lower bound. Whether $\kappa_{\text{ser}}$ has a theoretical lower bound under APC's serialized admission is not known.
9. Relation between PoS and the Byzantine $D_K$. We currently state only structural analogy. Whether any formal reduction exists is unresolved and is not pursued in this version.
10. Reproducibility package. Code, traces, PRML manifests, and analysis scripts are to be published before the confirmatory study.

---

11. Conclusion

AMR is framed here not as an architecture to be justified, but as a decision problem under constraints whose proposed solution is falsifiable. The theoretical apparatus:

· separates the mode-selection policy, the execution policy, and the authorization monitor, and includes history in the monitor signature;
· formalizes regret against reference classes with declared information structure, over fixed episodes, with clairvoyant values as expectations of maxima over realized episode paths;
· states security invariants as admissibility conditions, including P3c, and decomposes atomicity into three independently testable properties;
· provides explicit forbidden-history automata with regular-closure justification;
· inherits composition soundness from APC and delegation attenuation from CAPMAS rather than re-proving them;
· declares the serialization boundary as an explicit interface assumption with a measurable cost;
· specifies the mode-selection policy as a complete transition system with entry, exit, fallback, dwell, and hysteresis rules;
· adopts PRML v0.1 for single-metric threshold locks and OSF registration for the full statistical analysis plan;
· cites the security-regret decomposition as related work, not as formal grounding;
· positions the contribution as a conditional extension, not a novelty claim.

No experiments have been conducted. No models have been trained. No physical device tests have been performed. No code has been executed. All empirical claims are stated as hypotheses to be tested under the protocol of Section 7. All numerical thresholds are to be fixed by a future pilot study. All references to prior work are cited inline by author, year, and arXiv identifier.

The core epistemological stance is twofold: a system that cannot decline to adapt has not demonstrated adaptivity, and zero observed violations do not establish security — only bounded statistical confidence within a declared threat model, complementing formal verification within a declared formal model.

The value of the programme does not rest on AMR being superior in all regimes. It rests on a procedure that can show, with the same rigor, when it is not.

