Appendix A. Regularity and Closure of the Forbidden-History Family

A.1 Setup

Recall from Section 5.3 the declared alphabet

\Sigma = \{\, \text{read}(x, \ell),\; \text{send}(y, d),\; \text{delegate}(g),\; \text{exec}(t) \,\}.

A history is a finite word $h = a_1 a_2 \dots a_n \in \Sigma^*$. A forbidden-history family is a finite collection $\mathcal{F} = \{\mathcal{F}_1, \dots, \mathcal{F}_n\}$ where each $\mathcal{F}_i \subseteq \Sigma^*$ is recognized by a deterministic finite automaton (DFA).

A.2 Automaton $\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}$

Definition A.1. The exfiltration automaton is the DFA

\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}} = (Q_{\text{exfil}}, \Sigma, \delta_{\text{exfil}}, q_0^{\text{exfil}}, F_{\text{exfil}})

with state set $Q_{\text{exfil}} = \{q_0, q_1, q_2\}$, initial state $q_0$, accepting state $F_{\text{exfil}} = \{q_2\}$, and transition function $\delta_{\text{exfil}}$ defined by the following table. Let $\mathcal{L}_{\text{conf}} \subseteq \mathcal{X} \times \mathbb{N}$ be the set of confidentiality-labelled resources with $\ell \ge \text{conf}$, and let $\mathcal{D}_{\text{untrusted}}$ be the set of untrusted destinations.

State Input Next state
$q_0$ $\text{read}(x, \ell)$ with $(x, \ell) \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{conf}}$ $q_1$
$q_0$ any other symbol $q_0$
$q_1$ $\text{delegate}(g)$ $q_1$
$q_1$ $\text{send}(y, d)$ with $d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{untrusted}}$ $q_2$
$q_1$ $\text{read}(x', \ell')$ with $(x', \ell') \notin \mathcal{L}_{\text{conf}}$ $q_0$
$q_1$ any other symbol not listed above $q_0$
$q_2$ any symbol $q_2$

The accepting state $q_2$ is absorbing: once an exfiltration pattern has been recognized, the entire continuation is in $\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}$.

A.3 Automaton $\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$

Definition A.2. Let $B_{\max} \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$ be the root budget, fixed by the delegation tree. The budget-fragmentation automaton is the DFA

\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}} = (Q_{\text{budget}}, \Sigma, \delta_{\text{budget}}, q_0^{\text{budget}}, F_{\text{budget}})

with state set $Q_{\text{budget}} = \{q_0, q_1, \dots, q_{B_{\max}}, q_{\text{over}}\}$, initial state $q_0$, accepting state $F_{\text{budget}} = \{q_{\text{over}}\}$, and transition function:

State Input Next state
$q_c$ for $0 \le c \le B_{\max}$ $\text{delegate}(g)$ with budget $b_g$ and $c + b_g \le B_{\max}$ $q_{c + b_g}$
$q_c$ for $0 \le c \le B_{\max}$ $\text{delegate}(g)$ with budget $b_g$ and $c + b_g > B_{\max}$ $q_{\text{over}}$
$q_c$ for $0 \le c \le B_{\max}$ any other symbol $q_c$
$q_{\text{over}}$ any symbol $q_{\text{over}}$

The state $q_{\text{over}}$ is absorbing.

A.4 Regularity

Proposition A.1 (Finiteness). Both $\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}$ and $\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$ have finite state sets.

Proof. $|Q_{\text{exfil}}| = 3$, which is finite. $|Q_{\text{budget}}| = B_{\max} + 2$, which is finite because $B_{\max}$ is a fixed integer declared by the root budget. Since $B_{\max}$ is part of the declared model and not a runtime quantity, the state space is fixed at specification time. $\square$

Proposition A.2 (Regularity). $\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}) \subseteq \Sigma^*$ and $\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}) \subseteq \Sigma^*$ are regular languages.

Proof. A language is regular iff it is recognized by a DFA. By Proposition A.1, both automata are finite. By construction, both are deterministic: for each state and input, the transition table assigns at most one next state. Therefore both recognize regular languages. $\square$

Remark A.1 (Non-regular counterexample). If the budget were unbounded (i.e., $B_{\max}$ not fixed), $\mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$ would become a pushdown automaton with unbounded stack depth, recognizing a context-free but not necessarily regular language. The declared boundedness of $B_{\max}$ is therefore essential to regularity and must be part of the pre-registration.

A.5 Membership

Proposition A.3 (Linear-time membership). For any $h \in \Sigma^*$ with $|h| = n$, deciding whether $h \in \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{F}_i)$ for each $i$ takes $O(n)$ time.

Proof. A DFA processes each symbol exactly once, moving from one state to another in $O(1)$ per symbol. Processing $h$ symbol-by-symbol from $q_0$ yields the final state in $O(n)$ time. Membership holds iff the final state is accepting. $\square$

Corollary A.1. Membership of $h$ in $\mathcal{F} = \mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}} \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$ takes $O(n)$ time.

Proof. By Definition 5.1 clause (iv), we require $h_t \notin \mathcal{F}$, i.e., $h_t \notin \mathcal{F}_{\text{exfil}}$ and $h_t \notin \mathcal{F}_{\text{budget}}$. Running the two DFAs in parallel and rejecting as soon as either reaches an accepting state yields $O(n)$ time, since each DFA runs in $O(n)$ and constant work is added per symbol. $\square$

A.6 Closure under union

Proposition A.4 (Union closure). For any finite family $\{\mathcal{F}_1, \dots, \mathcal{F}_n\}$ of DFAs over $\Sigma$, the union $\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{F}_1) \cup \dots \cup \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{F}_n)$ is regular.

Proof. Standard result: regular languages are closed under finite union, witnessed by the product construction (or by NFA union followed by subset construction). Construct the NFA $N$ with a new initial state $q_{\text{new}}$ and $\varepsilon$-transitions to the initial states of each $\mathcal{F}_i$. The NFA recognizes the union; subset construction yields a DFA. $\square$

Remark A.2 (Practical note). The product construction for $n$ automata has state space $\prod_i |Q_i|$, which for two automata is $3 \times (B_{\max} + 2)$. Since $B_{\max}$ is fixed, this is bounded. For additional automata added in future versions, the state space remains a product of fixed sizes.

A.7 Alphabet and tool-parameter constraints

The alphabet $\Sigma$ is finite. Tool-parameter constraints — e.g., a specific tool $t$ restricted to a specific argument domain — are encoded as follows: the symbol $\text{exec}(t)$ is a family of symbols $\{\text{exec}(t, \theta) : \theta \in \Theta_t\}$ where $\Theta_t$ is a declared finite set of admissible arguments. If $\Theta_t$ is infinite (e.g., arbitrary strings), the automaton reads an abstracted symbol $\text{exec}(t, \text{category}(\theta))$ where $\text{category}(\cdot)$ maps arguments to a finite set of categories. The abstraction is part of the specification. Full-argument verification is delegated to the underlying tool-call layer, not to $\mathcal{F}$.

Remark A.3. This abstraction is a declared modeling boundary. Attacks that exploit within-category distinctions are outside the expressiveness of $\mathcal{F}$ and must be handled by the inherited CAPMAS semantic scoping layer.

