Appendix B. Nesting of Reference Classes

B.1 Statement

Recall Assumption 4.1: the reference classes satisfy

\mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}.

We establish the conditions under which this nesting holds.

B.2 Formal setting

Let $\mathcal{T}_e$ denote the set of admissible trajectories for episode $e$. A policy is a measurable mapping $\pi : \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{A}$ for the fixed policy case, or a sequence $\pi_{1:T} = (\pi_1, \dots, \pi_T)$ for adaptive cases. The switching cost between consecutive mode-selection policies is:

\kappa(\pi_t, \pi_{t+1}) = \begin{cases} \kappa_{\text{meta}}, & \text{if } \mu_t \neq \mu_{t+1}, \\ 0, & \text{if } \mu_t = \mu_{t+1}, \end{cases}

where $\kappa_{\text{meta}} > 0$ is the declared per-transition cost.

B.3 Proof of the first inclusion

Proposition B.1. $\mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}$ for any $K \ge 0$.

Proof. A policy $p \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}}$ is constant: $p = \pi$ for all $t$. Define $\tilde{p}_{1:T} = (\pi, \pi, \dots, \pi)$. Then $\tilde{p}_{1:T}$ performs zero mode transitions ($K = 0 \le K$), incurs zero switching cost, and satisfies full security constraints by assumption on $\mathcal{P}_{\text{fix}}$. Since $\mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}$ admits any causally-implementable policy with at most $K$ switches, $\tilde{p}_{1:T} \in \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}}$. $\square$

B.4 Proof of the second inclusion

Proposition B.2. $\mathcal{P}_K^{\text{causal}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}}$.

Proof. Every causally-implementable policy is also implementable by an agent with access to future information (a clairvoyant agent can simulate the causal policy by ignoring future information). Both classes share the same security constraints and the same bound $K$ on switches. Therefore causal $\subseteq$ clairvoyant-secured. $\square$

Remark B.1 (strictness). The inclusion is strict in general: there exist clairvoyant policies that outperform all causal policies at the same $K$ (e.g., policies that switch at the optimal moment, which is not knowable causally). Strictness under the AMR model is not a theorem but a consequence of the episode structure; it is stated as part of H2's empirical question.

B.5 Proof of the third inclusion

Proposition B.3. $\mathcal{P}_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}^{\text{clair,free}}$.

Proof. The clairvoyant-free class relaxes the security constraint (it is a superset of admissible security states) and relaxes the switch bound $K$ to unbounded. Therefore any secured-clairvoyant policy is admissible in the free-clairvoyant class. $\square$

B.6 Consequence

Corollary B.1. Under Propositions B.1–B.3, the optimal values satisfy

V_{\text{fix}}^\star \le V_K^{\text{causal}} \le V_K^{\text{clair,sec}} \le V^{\text{clair,free}}.

Therefore $\operatorname{PoS} \ge 0$, $\operatorname{PoC} \ge 0$, and $\operatorname{VoA} \ge 0$.

Proof. Immediate from monotonicity of the max functional under set inclusion of admissible policy classes. $\square$

Remark B.2 (interpretability caveat). Corollary B.1 is a mathematical statement about optimal values, not a claim about AMR's realized performance. Realized regret $R_\mu$ may be positive or negative depending on the quality of $\mu$; only the optimal values satisfy the chain above.

