Appendix D. Admissibility Set and Security Invariants: Formal Relations

D.1 Admissibility as a conjunction

Recall the admissibility predicate:

\pi \in \Pi_{\mathrm{adm}} \iff \mathrm{P1}(\pi) \wedge \mathrm{P2}(\pi) \wedge \mathrm{P3a}(\pi) \wedge \mathrm{P3b}(\pi) \wedge \mathrm{P3c}(\pi).

Each $\mathrm{P}i(\pi)$ is a property of the policy's induced execution trace.

D.2 Formalization of P1 — Complete mediation

Let $\mathcal{R}_{\text{prot}} \subseteq \mathcal{A}$ be the set of protected actions. Let $\text{Exec}_\pi(h)$ denote the set of actions executed by policy $\pi$ in history $h$.

\mathrm{P1}(\pi) \iff \forall h \in \mathcal{H}, \forall a \in \text{Exec}_\pi(h) : a \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{prot}} \Rightarrow \mathrm{Mon}(a, S(h), h) = \text{permit}.

D.3 Formalization of P2 — No escalation by composition (conditional)

Given Assumption 5.1 (inherited composition soundness from APC), P2 states:

\mathrm{P2}(\pi) \iff \forall \text{plan } P = \langle a_1, \dots, a_n \rangle, \forall S \subseteq \text{Agents}, \forall k : \mathrm{Exec}(a_k) \Rightarrow \mathrm{Permit}(a_k, S_k^{\text{monitor}}, h_k).

Proposition D.1. Under Assumption 5.1, P2 is satisfied by any policy whose protected actions pass through an APC implementation.

Proof. By Assumption 5.1, the APC substrate guarantees Composition Soundness under complete restriction set and serialized admission. A policy satisfying Assumption 3.1 (serialization boundary) invokes APC admission at every delegation boundary; therefore its executed actions satisfy the APC authorization predicate. $\square$

Remark D.1. Without Assumption 5.1, P2 is not derivable from Definition 5.1, which is non-compositional. This is stated explicitly in Section 5.5.

D.4 Formalization of P3a — Authorization coherence

Let $t_{\text{check}}(a)$ be the epoch at which action $a$ is authorized, and $t_{\text{commit}}(a)$ the epoch at which $a$ is irrevocably engaged.

\mathrm{P3a}(\pi) \iff \forall a \in \text{Exec}_\pi : t_{\text{check}}(a) < t_{\text{commit}}(a) \wedge \text{no update to } S^{\text{monitor}} \text{ in } [t_{\text{check}}, t_{\text{commit}}) \text{ invalidates } \mathrm{Permit}(a).

D.5 Formalization of P3b — At-most-once delivery

Let $\text{id}(a)$ be the operation identifier and $\text{recv}(a)$ the receiver. Let $\text{Effect}(a, \text{recv})$ denote the side effect.

\mathrm{P3b}(\pi) \iff \forall a, a' \in \text{Exec}_\pi : (\text{id}(a) = \text{id}(a') \wedge \text{recv}(a) = \text{recv}(a')) \Rightarrow a = a'.

D.6 Formalization of P3c — Revocation semantics

Let $\text{Rev}$ be a revocation event and $\text{Comm}(a)$ the committed status of action $a$. P3c is a declared modeling choice:

\mathrm{P3c}(\pi) \in \{ \text{block-uncommitted}, \text{affect-committed}, \text{both-with-compensation} \}.

Each choice is a predicate on the trace; the choice itself is part of the declared model.

D.7 Independence of P3a, P3b, P3c

Proposition D.2. P3a does not imply P3b.

Construction. Consider a remote tool call $a$ with id $= k$. Suppose the local authorization at $t_{\text{check}}$ succeeds and the local commit at $t_{\text{commit}} > t_{\text{check}}$ engages the call. Due to network retry, the request is sent twice. P3a holds because the local linearization was coherent. P3b fails because the remote receiver processes the same request twice. $\square$

Proposition D.3. P3b does not imply P3a.

Construction. Let id $= k$ be enforced by an idempotency key at the receiver. Suppose a revocation of the underlying delegation arrives at $t_{\text{rev}} \in (t_{\text{check}}, t_{\text{commit}})$. Without re-checking, the action commits. P3b holds (single effect at receiver). P3a fails (revocation was not respected at commit). $\square$

Proposition D.4. P3c is independent of P3a and P3b.

Justification. P3c is a declaration of which of the three revocation semantics applies. Under any semantics, both P3a and P3b may hold or fail independently; e.g., a system may block uncommitted operations upon revocation (P3c = block-uncommitted) while still exhibiting non-coherent linearization due to clock skew between monitor instances. $\square$

