Appendix E. Serialization Cost: Interface Between Parallel Execution and APC Admission

E.1 The interface problem

AMR's execution policy $\pi_{\text{exec}}$ may select a parallelism degree $d_t \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{exec}}$. The inherited APC substrate assumes serialized admission for its Composition Soundness theorem. If the execution policy selects $d_t > 1$, the interface breaks.

E.2 Formalization

Let $\mathcal{B}_t = \{b_1, \dots, b_k\}$ be the set of delegation boundaries active at epoch $t$. Let $t_{\text{adm}}(b_i)$ be the admission epoch of boundary $b_i$ under APC.

Assumption E.1 (Serialization boundary). Under APC, for any two boundaries $b_i, b_j$ active at the same epoch,

t_{\text{adm}}(b_i) \neq t_{\text{adm}}(b_j).

This is the serialized-admission condition.

E.3 Serialization cost

Define the serialization cost as:

\kappa_{\text{ser}}(t) = \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \Delta t_{\text{adm}}(b_{(i)}, b_{(i+1)}),

where $b_{(1)}, \dots, b_{(k)}$ are the boundaries ordered by admission epoch, and $\Delta t_{\text{adm}}$ is the admission-latency increment between consecutive boundaries.

Proposition E.1. Under serialized admission, the total latency of $k$ boundaries is

L_{\text{serial}}(k) = \sum_{i=1}^k \ell_{\text{adm}}(b_i),

where $\ell_{\text{adm}}(b_i)$ is the admission latency of $b_i$.

Under parallel admission with degree $d \le k$, the latency is

L_{\text{parallel}}(k, d) \approx \frac{1}{d} \sum_{i=1}^k \ell_{\text{adm}}(b_i),

assuming no contention on the underlying admission substrate.

Proof. Direct. $\square$

Corollary E.1 (Serialization penalty).

\kappa_{\text{ser}}(t) \approx L_{\text{serial}}(k) - L_{\text{parallel}}(k, d) = \left(1 - \frac{1}{d}\right) \sum_i \ell_{\text{adm}}(b_i).

Remark E.1 (Relation to APC's reported latency). APC reports admission latency of 0.24 ms at p99 on an idle host. Under serialization, this is paid per boundary. Under parallelism degree $d$, the amortized cost per boundary is $\ell_{\text{adm}} / d$. The serialization penalty is therefore a measurable quantity that must appear in H1's refutation criteria.

E.4 Interaction with utility

The serialization cost $\kappa_{\text{ser}}$ is a component of the price of security:

\operatorname{PoS} \ge \kappa_{\text{ser}} \cdot T_{\text{episode}} \cdot \mathbb{E}[\text{boundaries per epoch}],

in energy units, where $T_{\text{episode}}$ is the number of epochs.

Remark E.2. This is a lower bound, not the full PoS. The full PoS includes additional effects: refusal of actions blocked by $\mathcal{F}$, budget exhaustion, and revocation semantics.

E.5 Interface Assumption 3.1 (restated formally)

Assumption 3.1 (formally). For any epoch $t$ and any parallelism degree $d_t$ selected by $\pi_{\text{exec}}$, the APC admission protocol is invoked either (a) serially at every boundary, incurring $\kappa_{\text{ser}}(t)$, or (b) in parallel with degree at most $d_t$, satisfying Assumption E.1 by temporal separation of admission epochs implemented via the underlying substrate's mutual exclusion mechanism. Condition (b) is admissible only if the underlying substrate provides the mutual exclusion; otherwise, (a) applies.

