Appendix F. Estimator Specification for $\widehat{U}_t$

F.1 Short-horizon estimator

Let $\hat{c}_i(m)$ denote the observed correct-completion indicator for task $i$ under mode $m$, and analogously $\hat{e}_i(m), \hat{\ell}_i(m)$. Define the per-task utility:

\hat{u}_i(m) = \alpha \hat{c}_i(m) - \beta \hat{e}_i(m) - \gamma \hat{\ell}_i(m).

The short-horizon estimate is the exponentially weighted moving average with window $N_{\text{short}} = 20$:

\widehat{U}^{\text{short}}_t(m) = \frac{\sum_{j=0}^{N_{\text{short}}-1} w_j \hat{u}_{t-j}(m)}{\sum_{j=0}^{N_{\text{short}}-1} w_j}, \quad w_j = (1 - \rho)^j,

with decay rate $\rho \in (0, 1)$ fixed on the pilot.

F.2 Long-horizon estimator

Let $\bar{u}(m)$ be the pilot-derived mean per-task utility. The long-horizon estimate is a linear rollout:

\widehat{U}^{\text{long}}_t(m) = \bar{u}(m) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{t - \tau_{\text{entry}}}{T_{\text{episode}}}\right) + \widehat{U}^{\text{short}}_t(m) \cdot \frac{t - \tau_{\text{entry}}}{T_{\text{episode}}}.

This is a linear interpolation between the pilot prior (at mode entry) and the observed short-horizon estimate (as the episode progresses). The interpolation weights are declared, not learned.

F.3 Uncertainty estimator

Let $\{\widehat{U}^{(j)}_t(m)\}_{j=1}^{N_{\text{ens}}}$ be estimates from an ensemble of $N_{\text{ens}} = 5$ seed-varying predictors. The uncertainty is:

\text{uncertainty}_t(m) = \frac{1}{N_{\text{ens}}} \sum_{j=1}^{N_{\text{ens}}} \left( \widehat{U}^{(j)}_t(m) - \overline{U}_t(m) \right)^2, \quad \overline{U}_t(m) = \frac{1}{N_{\text{ens}}} \sum_{j=1}^{N_{\text{ens}}} \widehat{U}^{(j)}_t(m).

F.4 Fallback condition

The fallback rule of Section 3.1 triggers when:

\text{uncertainty}_t(m) > \upsilon_{\max} \quad \text{or} \quad t - \tau_{\text{entry}} > H_{\text{stale}},

where $H_{\text{stale}}$ is the declared staleness horizon.

F.5 Commitment

The tuple $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \rho, N_{\text{short}}, N_{\text{ens}}, \upsilon_{\max}, H_{\text{stale}}, \bar{u}(\cdot))$ is fixed on the pilot and committed via SHA-256 before the confirmatory study. Any post-commitment change invalidates H_abstain, H_cost, and H1.

