Appendix K. Threats to Validity: Formal Statements

Each threat is stated as a null hypothesis against which the protocol tests.

K.1 Orchestration overhead

\text{Threat}_{\text{overhead}} : \mathbb{E}[\text{orchestration energy}] > \eta \cdot \mathbb{E}[\text{total energy}],

for a pre-registered fraction $\eta$ fixed on the pilot. If not rejected, AMR's gain cannot exceed its cost regardless of allocation quality.

K.2 Thermal noise

\text{Threat}_{\text{thermal}} : \text{Var}(\text{latency} \mid \text{thermal throttling}) \gg \text{Var}(\text{latency} \mid \text{no throttling}),

which collapses statistical power. Controlled by randomization and thermal stabilization.

K.3 Strong baselines

\text{Threat}_{\text{baseline}} : \mu_{\text{AMR}} \le \mu_{\text{HeRo}} \vee \mu_{\text{AMR}} \le \mu_{\text{DAOEF}},

on the corresponding axes. Non-rejection of this null is a legitimate outcome.

K.4 Serialization cost

\text{Threat}_{\text{serial}} : \kappa_{\text{ser}} \cdot T_{\text{episode}} \cdot \mathbb{E}[\text{boundaries}] > \operatorname{VoA}.

If not rejected, the security overhead annihilates the adaptation gain.

K.5 Utility misspecification

\text{Threat}_{\text{utility}} : \widehat{U} \text{ differs from the true utility by more than } \varepsilon_{\text{spec}}.

If the pilot does not establish a bound on $\varepsilon_{\text{spec}}$, the falsifiability of H_abstain, H_cost, and H1 is voided.

K.6 Simulator calibration

\text{Threat}_{\text{calib}} : \text{residual}(\text{simulator}, \text{hardware}) > \varepsilon_{\text{calib}}.

If not rejected, oracle regrets are not admissible as evidence for H1 or H2.

