Appendix M. Proof of the Serialization Lower Bound (Conditional)

Proposition M.1 (conditional). Under Assumption E.1 (serialized admission) and Assumption 3.1 (interface condition), the serialization cost satisfies:

\kappa_{\text{ser}} \ge \ell_{\text{adm}}^{\min} \cdot (k - 1),

where $\ell_{\text{adm}}^{\min} > 0$ is the minimum admission latency and $k$ is the number of active boundaries.

Proof. By Assumption E.1, admission epochs are pairwise distinct. By the definition of admission latency, each consecutive pair contributes at least $\ell_{\text{adm}}^{\min}$ of latency. With $k$ boundaries, there are $k - 1$ consecutive pairs, each contributing at least $\ell_{\text{adm}}^{\min}$. $\square$

Remark M.1. The bound is tight only when all $k$ boundaries share the same admission latency $\ell_{\text{adm}}^{\min}$. In practice, admission latency may vary across boundary types; the bound remains valid but is conservative.

---

Appendix N. Proof of Independence of $K_{\text{meta}}$ and $K_{\text{exec}}$

Proposition N.1. The counters $K_{\text{meta}}$ and $K_{\text{exec}}$ are not functions of each other.

Construction. Consider an episode in which the mode-selection policy remains in $m_{\text{adapt}}$ throughout ($K_{\text{meta}} = 0$), but the execution policy alternates placement between CPU and NPU on every epoch ($K_{\text{exec}} = T$). Conversely, consider an episode in which the mode-selection policy oscillates between $m_{\text{simple}}$ and $m_{\text{adapt}}$ every epoch ($K_{\text{meta}} = T$), while the execution policy within $m_{\text{adapt}}$ selects the same placement throughout ($K_{\text{exec}} = 0$ within that mode). Both configurations are admissible. $\square$

Remark N.1 (implication for regret). Reporting only $K_{\text{meta}}$ conflates a stable system with a well-behaved one. A system with low $K_{\text{meta}}$ but high $K_{\text{exec}}$ incurs continuous migration energy that would be invisible under the meta-only accounting. This is the reason Section 4.3 declares both counters and reports sensitivity to both.

